[{"id":"fb-p01","sourceId":"P01","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Ingestion design","title":"Separate movement from transformation","prompt":"A nightly flow copies ERP rows, joins a reference table, computes grouped totals in Power Query, and runs a validation notebook only after preparation succeeds. The team wants low-code ownership for the joins. Which design meets all requirements with the fewest custom components?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use Copy for ingestion, then a notebook for the joins and aggregation, followed by a second validation notebook.","why":"This can implement the operations, but moves the analyst-owned Power Query preparation into custom notebook code."},{"id":"B","text":"Use a pipeline to orchestrate Copy, Dataflow Gen2, and Notebook activities with success dependencies.","why":"Each engine performs its supported work, while the pipeline enforces the required completion order."},{"id":"C","text":"Use a Dataflow Gen2 transformation and schedule the notebook five minutes later without a dependency.","why":"The transformation is suitable, but a delay cannot ensure it completes successfully before validation."},{"id":"D","text":"Run Copy, Dataflow Gen2, and the validation notebook on separate recurring schedules with increasing start-time offsets.","why":"The engines are suitable, but independent schedules do not establish successful completion dependencies."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Choose the preparation engine and orchestration separately. A pipeline coordinates work; Copy alone is not a join-and-aggregate engine.","videoSeconds":125,"refs":["pcopy","pactivity","movement"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p02","sourceId":"P02","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Copy configuration","title":"Preserve artifacts and parse rows","prompt":"Invoices arrive as PDFs; a CSV lists invoice_id and amount. You must preserve each PDF byte-for-byte in Files and load typed CSV rows into a Lakehouse table. Select TWO configuration decisions.","type":"multi","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use Binary for the PDF transfer between file locations.","why":"Binary preserves the artifact without interpreting it as rows or requiring a tabular schema."},{"id":"B","text":"Use Binary for the CSV-to-table transfer and map its second column to decimal.","why":"Binary does not expose CSV fields for mapping; the tabular input must be parsed."},{"id":"C","text":"Use Delimited text for the CSV, validate its parser settings, and configure the table mapping.","why":"Parsing exposes named fields so the table load can apply the intended column types."},{"id":"D","text":"Use Delimited text for the PDFs so that Copy can infer invoice totals from their rendered pages.","why":"A delimited-text parser does not extract semantic fields from rendered PDF pages."}],"correct":["A","C"],"explanation":"The same Copy activity family serves different contracts. File preservation and parsed table ingestion require different source-format choices.","videoSeconds":1013,"refs":["pcopy","plake"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p03","sourceId":"P03","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Copy configuration","title":"Build a typed snapshot load","prompt":"You have an immutable UTF-8 CSV with a header in Lakehouse Files. A new Delta table must contain only its rows, with year as integer. A downstream notebook must not start on failed ingestion. Arrange these four steps.","type":"sequence","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure the Tables destination, table action, and explicit column/type mapping.","why":"The parsed source schema is needed to establish the intended destination types and write behavior."},{"id":"B","text":"Select Files as the source, configure Delimited text and header handling, and preview the parsed rows.","why":"Verify the input interpretation before configuring how those fields become table columns."},{"id":"C","text":"Inspect Copy output and the loaded table, then release downstream processing on success.","why":"Verification and downstream release follow the completed copy, not merely its configuration."},{"id":"D","text":"Save and execute the pipeline containing the configured Copy activity.","why":"Execution applies the validated configuration and produces the run evidence to inspect."}],"correct":["B","A","D","C"],"explanation":"Parse first, establish the target contract, execute, then inspect the outcome. This is a practical workflow, not a claim that authoring tabs enforce this order.","videoSeconds":973,"refs":["plake","pcopy","pmonitor"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p04","sourceId":"P04","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Copy configuration","title":"Choose an appropriate preparation surface","prompt":"A supported CSV-to-Lakehouse copy must rename movie_year to year and parse numeric years, but another requirement computes average rating by director after joining a lookup table. Select TWO sound decisions.","type":"multi","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the Copy Mapping tab to express the multi-table join and GROUP BY.","why":"Mapping associates fields and supported types; it is not the requested relational aggregation surface."},{"id":"B","text":"Keep every field as text because Copy cannot perform any type conversion.","why":"Supported parsed copies can apply field mappings and type conversions."},{"id":"C","text":"Use Copy's supported mapping for the rename and numeric conversion.","why":"These are column-level operations compatible with the stated parsed-copy configuration."},{"id":"D","text":"Perform the join and aggregate in Dataflow Gen2 or an appropriate notebook after ingestion.","why":"The rowset operation belongs to a transformation engine, orchestrated when its input is ready."}],"correct":["C","D"],"explanation":"Distinguish column-level representation changes from rowset logic. Confirm destination type support and validate actual values before accepting the load.","videoSeconds":1148,"refs":["pcopy","plake","pactivity"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p05","sourceId":"P05","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Copy configuration","title":"Diagnose a quoted delimiter","prompt":"A CSV header has three fields. A movie title includes a comma inside double quotes; the year column sometimes appears shifted in preview. The destination schema is correct. What should you inspect first?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Adjust the destination year mapping and culture settings before repeating the same source preview.","why":"The destination schema is already correct; type mapping cannot repair fields split incorrectly in source preview."},{"id":"B","text":"Disable quote handling and increase skipped source lines until the header preview contains three columns.","why":"Skipping lines does not repair embedded delimiters in data rows; disabling quote handling can split the quoted title."},{"id":"C","text":"Change the success dependency to Completion so incorrectly parsed rows can load.","why":"Execution dependencies do not repair source parsing and may release failed preparation."},{"id":"D","text":"Check delimiter, quote/escape, and header settings against the file; repeat preview before loading.","why":"The observed column shift is a parsing issue, so validate input interpretation before writing."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"A destination schema cannot compensate for fields split incorrectly at the source. Diagnose the parsed preview before tuning transport or execution order.","videoSeconds":1030,"refs":["pformat","pcopy"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p06","sourceId":"P06","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Load correctness","title":"Derive a replayed snapshot result","prompt":"The target initially holds 120 rows. Snapshot A has 90 rows. A replay of A must leave exactly those 90 rows. Snapshot B later has 85 rows and includes source deletions. Which table action meets the complete-snapshot requirement?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Overwrite on every snapshot; final counts are 90, 90, and 85.","why":"Replacement reflects the entire current snapshot and prevents accumulation on replay."},{"id":"B","text":"Append on every snapshot; final counts are 90, 90, and 85.","why":"Append retains prior rows and adds each input again; it cannot produce these counts."},{"id":"C","text":"Append only when a new RunId is observed; this always detects a replayed business batch.","why":"A new run can have a new RunId even when it processes the same business snapshot."},{"id":"D","text":"Upsert by key without delete handling; removed source keys are automatically absent.","why":"Insert/update alone retains destination keys omitted from a complete snapshot."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"Given a full snapshot contract, replacement removes stale rows as well as duplicates. The example assumes complete, validated input and no concurrent destination writer.","videoSeconds":1134,"refs":["plake"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p07","sourceId":"P07","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Load correctness","title":"Reject an unsafe incremental replacement","prompt":"A target contains twelve months of orders. Today's file contains only new orders; historical rows must remain. A developer copies the video's Overwrite setting into this load. What is the best correction?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Keep Overwrite because every Delta write retains current historical rows automatically.","why":"Historical versions do not make overwritten rows part of the current table state."},{"id":"B","text":"Keep Overwrite and reduce retries to zero so the historical data stays present.","why":"Retry count does not alter the replacement semantics of the first execution."},{"id":"C","text":"Use a load strategy that preserves history and handles replay, rather than replacing the entire table with the delta file.","why":"The incremental file is not a full snapshot; its write and retry contract must preserve existing rows."},{"id":"D","text":"Use Binary so the same Overwrite setting becomes an automatic row merge.","why":"A file-format choice does not turn replacement into a merge or parse tabular keys."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"The video's action serves its small full-snapshot demo. An incremental load needs a different correctness contract; transport success alone cannot prove it.","videoSeconds":1137,"refs":["plake","pincrement"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p08","sourceId":"P08","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Connections","title":"Read the capability matrix","prompt":"The supplied connector matrix lists SQL Server pipeline Copy as source-only, but lists Script and Stored procedure activities as supported. You need to write transformed rows back to SQL Server. Which conclusion follows?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Because Copy can read SQL Server, it can also use SQL Server as a Copy destination.","why":"Source support does not imply destination support for the same activity."},{"id":"B","text":"Use a supported writing mechanism, such as authorized SQL logic through a supported activity, and verify its contract separately.","why":"The matrix distinguishes activity support; a supported SQL execution route can differ from a Copy sink route."},{"id":"C","text":"No pipeline activity can write to the database because Copy lacks destination support.","why":"That conclusion ignores the separately supported SQL execution activities."},{"id":"D","text":"Creating an additional connection converts the connector into a supported Copy sink.","why":"Connection credentials do not change the connector's documented capability matrix."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Check the particular tool, direction, activity, gateway, and authentication combination. This question supplies the matrix so it does not depend on memorizing an evolving connector list.","videoSeconds":379,"refs":["psql","pconnect"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p09","sourceId":"P09","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Evaluate an inactive success","prompt":"Copy A is deactivated and explicitly marked Succeeded. B depends on A Succeeded; C on A Failed. B and C are valid independent Wait activities. Which work actually executes?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"A and B execute; C is skipped.","why":"B is eligible, but A is inactive and performs no copy work."},{"id":"B","text":"Only C executes because an inactive copy is treated as failed.","why":"The explicitly assigned Succeeded state governs branching, not a presumed failure."},{"id":"C","text":"Only B executes; A does no copy work and C does not execute.","why":"The success branch is eligible; the inactive predecessor only supplies its assigned branching state."},{"id":"D","text":"Nothing executes because inactivity forces every descendant to be skipped.","why":"Assigned inactive state can activate downstream branches even though no upstream work occurred."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"Separate physical execution from the state used for dependency evaluation. Assigned success is not evidence that the expected data was copied.","videoSeconds":1615,"refs":["pactivity","pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p10","sourceId":"P10","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Evaluate an inactive skip","prompt":"A is deactivated and marked Skipped. B depends on A Completed and C on A Skipped. Both downstream activities are otherwise valid. Which branch is eligible?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Only C; Completed does not include Skipped.","why":"The skip branch matches the assigned state; Completion covers success or failure."},{"id":"B","text":"B and C; Completed means any final status.","why":"A skipped predecessor does not satisfy a Completion dependency."},{"id":"C","text":"Neither; a deactivated predecessor cannot release a downstream branch.","why":"Assigned inactive state can activate a matching branch even without actual predecessor execution."},{"id":"D","text":"Only B; the skip branch is available only after an actual execution.","why":"Assigned inactive Skipped state can activate the skip branch without predecessor execution."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"A final UI row and an eligible Completion dependency are different concepts. Follow the configured branch state.","videoSeconds":1599,"refs":["pactivity","pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p11","sourceId":"P11","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Trace a mixed dependency chain","prompt":"A executes successfully. B depends on A Failed. C depends on B Skipped. D depends on B Completed. C and D have no other dependencies and would succeed if run. Which outcome follows?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"B fails, then C and D execute.","why":"B does not execute, so it is skipped rather than failed; Completion is not satisfied."},{"id":"B","text":"B and C are skipped; D executes.","why":"C's skip dependency is satisfied, while D's Completion dependency is not."},{"id":"C","text":"B, C, and D all execute because A succeeded.","why":"A's success does not satisfy B's failure condition or D's condition on B."},{"id":"D","text":"B is skipped, C executes, and D is skipped.","why":"Trace each edge against its immediate predecessor's state rather than propagating A's success."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"Skipped can be the result of an unmet condition, not an execution error. Evaluate one dependency at a time.","videoSeconds":1525,"refs":["pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p12","sourceId":"P12","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Gate a two-source publication","prompt":"Independent loads A and B supply two required input tables. Validation V must run only if both loads succeed. A succeeds and B fails. Which graph correctly prevents validation?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"V has a Succeeded dependency on A and a separate Completed dependency on B.","why":"B's failure satisfies Completed, incorrectly permitting validation with a failed required input."},{"id":"B","text":"V has separate Succeeded dependencies on both A and B.","why":"Both required success conditions must hold; B's failure makes V ineligible."},{"id":"C","text":"V has a Succeeded dependency only on A because A starts after B is scheduled.","why":"Scheduling order does not establish successful completion of the other input."},{"id":"D","text":"V is an independent activity with a Wait delay equal to B's average run time.","why":"An estimated delay cannot establish B's successful completion."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Use dependencies on every required input. The target condition is both loads succeeding, not at least one load finishing.","videoSeconds":147,"refs":["pdefinition","pincrement"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p13","sourceId":"P13","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Place audit on the correct predecessor","prompt":"Copy A actually runs and fails. An unrelated optional branch B is skipped. Audit must run after A succeeds or fails, and must not depend on B doing any work. A has no other outgoing dependencies. What is the correct dependency?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Audit depends on B Completed.","why":"B is skipped and cannot satisfy Completion in the failed-copy path."},{"id":"B","text":"Audit depends on B Succeeded.","why":"That condition would omit the path where Copy fails and B is skipped."},{"id":"C","text":"Audit depends on A Completed.","why":"A has a real success-or-failure outcome, exactly matching the audit requirement."},{"id":"D","text":"Audit depends on A Skipped.","why":"A executed and failed; the skip condition does not match."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"Place the audit dependency at the stage whose outcome you need to record. A skip further downstream can break a poorly placed Completion branch.","videoSeconds":1510,"refs":["pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p14","sourceId":"P14","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dependency reasoning","title":"Do not read nonexistent inactive output","prompt":"An inactive Copy marked Succeeded releases a validation activity. Validation reads @activity('CopyA').output.rowsCopied and fails because that field is absent. What is the best explanation?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"The assigned state controls branching but does not create Copy's normal output fields.","why":"Inactive execution has no actual copied-row output; downstream logic must not assume otherwise."},{"id":"B","text":"Marking it Succeeded guarantees the output field exists with value zero.","why":"The branching state does not fabricate a normal activity output contract."},{"id":"C","text":"The expression fails only because pipeline parameters cannot contain numeric values.","why":"The missing activity field is unrelated to parameter numeric types."},{"id":"D","text":"Disabling Secure output causes an inactive Copy to perform its data transfer.","why":"Logging settings cannot activate a deactivated activity or generate its transfer metrics."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"An inactive placeholder is useful for development, but it cannot supply evidence that validates a real load. Restore execution or change the test path explicitly.","videoSeconds":1610,"refs":["pactivity"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p15","sourceId":"P15","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Recovery","title":"Distinguish attempts from retries","prompt":"An activity's explicit retry value is 3, using Fixed retry interval = 30 seconds. Each attempt lasts exactly 10 seconds and fails transiently; no timeout or conditional retry policy intervenes. Ignore orchestration overhead. How many attempts occur, and how much total elapsed time passes before its failure branch becomes eligible?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"3 attempts and 90 seconds.","why":"Three retries are additional to the initial attempt; this omits one attempt."},{"id":"B","text":"4 attempts and 160 seconds.","why":"There are only three waiting intervals between four attempts, not four intervals."},{"id":"C","text":"3 attempts and 120 seconds.","why":"This treats retry count as total attempts and counts a nonexistent final wait."},{"id":"D","text":"4 attempts and 130 seconds.","why":"The total is four ten-second attempts plus three thirty-second waits."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"Read the explicit policy rather than relying on a UI default. The failure dependency is evaluated after the configured attempts are exhausted.","videoSeconds":923,"refs":["pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p16","sourceId":"P16","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Recovery","title":"Represent a business validation failure","prompt":"Copy completes without a transport error, but an authorized validation query reports 17 duplicate order keys. No downstream consumer may receive this batch, and the run must explicitly fail with a business error code. Which design meets the requirement?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Treat Copy's Succeeded status as proof that every business quality rule passed.","why":"Transport success does not establish uniqueness or other domain-specific constraints."},{"id":"B","text":"Evaluate validation output, route invalid data to a Fail activity, and release consumers only on validated success.","why":"Explicit business validation and failure handling enforce both the consumer gate and diagnostic outcome."},{"id":"C","text":"Connect the consumer directly to validation Completed so it runs after the query ends.","why":"Completion also allows a failure path and does not imply the validation predicate passed."},{"id":"D","text":"Rename the Copy activity to Failed when duplicates are reported.","why":"An activity's display name does not create the required failure outcome or control flow."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Readiness is a business decision after successful movement. Give invalid batches an explicit failure path instead of a reassuring transport status.","videoSeconds":1275,"refs":["pfail","plookup"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p17","sourceId":"P17","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Separate immutable inputs from runtime state","prompt":"A reusable pipeline receives source_file and target_table from its caller. It also records a mutable processing-stage flag during execution. Select TWO sound choices.","type":"multi","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Define the source and target as pipeline parameters and reference their run values.","why":"Caller-controlled inputs remain fixed for that execution and support reuse across runs."},{"id":"B","text":"Define the source and target as pipeline variables and expect the caller's same-named parameters to populate them automatically.","why":"Same names do not create automatic binding; caller inputs need explicit parameter contracts and references."},{"id":"C","text":"Store mutable execution state in an appropriate pipeline variable.","why":"Variables represent runtime state distinct from the caller's fixed inputs."},{"id":"D","text":"Use each parameter's authored default even when the caller supplies an explicit value for that run.","why":"Defaults provide fallback values; they do not take precedence over explicitly supplied run inputs."}],"correct":["A","C"],"explanation":"Identify whether a value is an input contract or changing internal state. Similar-looking values do not make parameters and variables interchangeable.","videoSeconds":1326,"refs":["pparams","pexpr"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p18","sourceId":"P18","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Use the caller's file name","prompt":"The parent supplies file_name = 'orders_2026-10-10.csv'. A downstream Copy Source file-name field supports expressions and must use exactly this value. Which expression references the supplied input?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"@variables('file_name')","why":"This reads a pipeline variable; the scenario defines a parameter instead."},{"id":"B","text":"@concat('archive/', pipeline().parameters.file_name)","why":"This prepends a folder to a field that must contain exactly the supplied filename."},{"id":"C","text":"@replace(pipeline().parameters.file_name, '.csv', '.parquet')","why":"This changes the caller's filename rather than using its exact supplied value."},{"id":"D","text":"@pipeline().parameters.file_name","why":"This directly references the pipeline parameter evaluated for this run."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"Bind the field to the parameter contract rather than hardcoding a filename or referencing a different namespace.","videoSeconds":1413,"refs":["pparams"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p19","sourceId":"P19","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Preserve a numeric expression type","prompt":"A custom activity field requires a numeric value. The Int parameter batch_size has value 8. You must preserve its numeric type instead of producing text. Which expression meets the contract?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"@{pipeline().parameters.batch_size}","why":"Interpolation produces a string even when the parameter is numeric."},{"id":"B","text":"@pipeline().parameters.batch_size","why":"The whole-field expression returns the numeric parameter value."},{"id":"C","text":"@concat('', string(pipeline().parameters.batch_size))","why":"Concatenation creates a string; the destination contract explicitly requires a number."},{"id":"D","text":"batch_size","why":"A bare parameter name is a literal here, not a parameter expression."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Whole expressions and interpolated strings can look similar while returning different types. Evaluate the receiving property's type contract.","videoSeconds":1405,"refs":["pparams","pexpr"],"code":"Parameter: batch_size (Int) = 8\nRequired result type: number","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p20","sourceId":"P20","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Read the supplied Lookup shape","prompt":"GetTargets uses First row only = false and returns the shown output. ForEach Items must receive the array of target records, and its child Copy must use the current record's table_name. Which pair of expressions is correct?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Items: @activity('GetTargets').output.firstRow; table: @item().table_name","why":"The supplied array output has no firstRow; this references the wrong output shape."},{"id":"B","text":"Items: @activity('GetTargets').output.count; table: @variables('table_name')","why":"count is not the record array; a shared variable is not the current record."},{"id":"C","text":"Items: @activity('GetTargets').output.value; table: @item().table_name","why":"value supplies the records, and item() selects the current iteration's record."},{"id":"D","text":"Items: @pipeline().parameters.GetTargets; table: @activity('GetTargets').output.table_name","why":"Neither expression follows the activity output and array-record contract provided."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"Use the documented output shape actually produced, then access the iterator record. Do not mix array and first-row Lookup modes.","videoSeconds":402,"refs":["plookup","pforeach","pexpr"],"code":"{\"count\":2,\"value\":[{\"table_name\":\"orders\"},{\"table_name\":\"customers\"}]}","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p21","sourceId":"P21","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Eliminate a shared-name race","prompt":"A parallel ForEach first sets a pipeline variable current_table from @item().table_name, then invokes a child load using that variable. Sometimes two iterations load the same table. You must keep parallelism. What is the strongest correction?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Pass @item().table_name directly as the child input and keep per-entity runtime state within the child invocation.","why":"The iteration's own record supplies its immutable input without racing on shared parent state."},{"id":"B","text":"Increase the parent variable's string length so writes cannot overwrite each other.","why":"Storage length does not isolate concurrently changing shared state."},{"id":"C","text":"Insert a fixed Wait before reading current_table in each iteration.","why":"A delay cannot guarantee which iteration last changed a shared value."},{"id":"D","text":"Rename current_table to a pipeline parameter and continue changing it with Set Variable.","why":"A parameter cannot be mutated using Set Variable during the run."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"Parallel iterations need isolated inputs. Direct iterator values avoid the mutable parent variable entirely.","videoSeconds":408,"refs":["pforeach","pexpr","pparams"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p22","sourceId":"P22","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Derive bounded concurrent execution","prompt":"Five independent entities take 2, 7, 3, 6, and 4 minutes. A ForEach uses Batch count = 2. Each child call waits for completion; targets do not overlap. Which statement is guaranteed by this configuration?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Every pair starts at the same instant and the exact total duration is 11 minutes.","why":"A concurrency cap alone does not specify a scheduling order or exact runtime."},{"id":"B","text":"All five entities start immediately because each child is a separate pipeline.","why":"The waiting calls remain constrained by the parent's ForEach concurrency cap."},{"id":"C","text":"One entity must run at a time, even though Sequential is not selected.","why":"Nonsequential execution permits multiple items within the configured batch cap."},{"id":"D","text":"At most two entity calls are active together; exact elapsed time requires execution evidence.","why":"Batch count bounds concurrent items, while actual scheduling and resource behavior determine duration."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"Do not turn a maximum concurrency setting into a guarantee about scheduling, speedup, or exactly how many items run at every moment.","videoSeconds":408,"refs":["pforeach","pperf"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p23","sourceId":"P23","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Use the correct replay identity","prompt":"A storage event causes a load; the same business file event can be retried through a new pipeline run. You need an audit field that groups attempts for that batch and another that identifies each pipeline run. Which pair is appropriate?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use RunId for both; new run IDs are automatically identical for the same file.","why":"RunId identifies an execution, not a stable business batch across different executions."},{"id":"B","text":"Use a supplied stable batch_id for grouping and @pipeline().RunId for individual attempts.","why":"The business identity correlates replays, while the system run identity distinguishes executions."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the activity's display name for both identities.","why":"A reused display name does not uniquely identify a batch or an execution."},{"id":"D","text":"Use current UTC time rounded to the hour as the unique batch identity.","why":"Distinct batches can share an hour and the same batch can retry in a later hour."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Define a business key independently of an execution key. A filename or run timestamp is adequate only if its uniqueness contract is explicitly guaranteed.","videoSeconds":475,"refs":["pparams","pruns"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p24","sourceId":"P24","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Dynamic pipelines","title":"Pass a notebook input explicitly","prompt":"A notebook contains a designated parameter cell with batch_id = 'demo'. Production invokes it from a pipeline whose batch_id parameter is 'B-42'. The notebook still uses 'demo'. What should you configure?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Update the notebook parameter cell's default to B-42 and leave the activity's base parameters unset for future batches.","why":"This hardcodes one batch instead of binding the notebook input to each production run."},{"id":"B","text":"Add batch_id to the parent pipeline's parameters and rely on matching names to pass it into the notebook.","why":"Matching names alone do not pass a value across the activity boundary; configure its base parameter binding."},{"id":"C","text":"Set or override the Notebook activity's base parameter batch_id from the pipeline parameter.","why":"The explicit activity input bridges the pipeline's parameter and the notebook's parameter cell."},{"id":"D","text":"Change the notebook's source table name to @pipeline().parameters.batch_id inside Python.","why":"The pipeline expression language is not evaluated automatically as arbitrary Python code."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"A parameter cell enables an input contract; it does not by itself bind every same-named pipeline parameter. Review the actual activity input.","videoSeconds":306,"refs":["pnotebook","pparams"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p25","sourceId":"P25","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Operations","title":"Configure a seasonal local schedule","prompt":"A pipeline must run at 08:00 Europe/London local time throughout the year, within an explicit operating period. UTC offsets change seasonally. Which schedule is most appropriate?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Select the supported Europe/London time zone, configure 08:00 and valid start/end dates, and enable the schedule.","why":"A named time zone expresses the business clock without manually hardcoding one seasonal UTC offset."},{"id":"B","text":"Create one daily schedule at 08:00 UTC and another at 07:00 UTC, both enabled throughout the operating period.","why":"Both remain active, causing two daily runs instead of one at the required local time."},{"id":"C","text":"Keep the schedule disabled and rely on the pipeline's creation date to start it daily.","why":"An unconfigured or disabled schedule does not satisfy recurring execution."},{"id":"D","text":"Configure an enabled daily 08:00 schedule in a fixed UTC-offset zone matching London's offset when it is created.","why":"A fixed offset does not follow London's seasonal clock changes."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"Represent a recurring business clock in the schedule, not in an arbitrary delay. Validate the chosen time zone and activation period.","videoSeconds":477,"refs":["pruns"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p26","sourceId":"P26","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Operations","title":"Avoid a guessed readiness delay","prompt":"Files arrive between 01:00 and 04:00, and transformation takes a variable duration. A report must refresh after the relevant file arrives and its processing validates. Which design enforces the requirement?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Run the report refresh at 02:00 and assume the usual arrival distribution is sufficient.","why":"Averages cannot guarantee the required batch has arrived and validated."},{"id":"B","text":"Trigger from the relevant arrival event and chain load, transformation, validation, and release through the required success conditions.","why":"Event initiation and dependent stages establish readiness from the actual batch lifecycle."},{"id":"C","text":"Use a fixed three-hour Wait after a 01:00 run instead of checking arrival or validation.","why":"A delay can expire without the file or a valid transformed result."},{"id":"D","text":"Connect refresh to load Completed so it runs whether load succeeds or fails.","why":"That allows consumer release after a failed required load."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Events answer when to start; dependencies answer when downstream work is eligible. They solve separate parts of the readiness problem.","videoSeconds":839,"refs":["pruns","pdefinition"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p27","sourceId":"P27","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Operations","title":"Interpret conversion metrics","prompt":"A parsed CSV-to-Delta load succeeds with rowsRead = rowsCopied = 100,000, rowsSkipped = 0, but dataRead differs from dataWritten. You must decide whether bytes alone prove corruption. What is the best conclusion?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"The data is corrupted because every successful copy must preserve identical byte counts.","why":"Different representations and compression can have different byte sizes for the same logical rows."},{"id":"B","text":"The data is certainly correct because Succeeded eliminates the need for business checks.","why":"Execution success and counts are useful evidence, but they do not prove every business value is correct."},{"id":"C","text":"The copy performed no conversion because equal row counts imply identical physical files.","why":"Logical row count does not establish identical physical representation."},{"id":"D","text":"Different byte sizes are compatible with format conversion; reconcile row counts and validate business values separately.","why":"Use representation-aware metrics and domain validation instead of treating byte equality as a universal rule."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"Monitoring describes an execution. Validation determines whether the resulting data meets the required contract.","videoSeconds":1275,"refs":["pmonitor"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p28","sourceId":"P28","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Operations","title":"Estimate a throughput ceiling","prompt":"Assume independent sustained limits: source 70 MB/s, destination 45 MB/s, network 30 MB/s. A new setting doubles copy compute capacity but changes none of these limits. Ignoring overhead, which end-to-end ceiling is defensible?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"140 MB/s because source throughput doubles with compute.","why":"The scenario explicitly leaves source and all external bottleneck limits unchanged."},{"id":"B","text":"45 MB/s because the destination is always the slowest stage.","why":"The network's stated limit is lower than the destination's."},{"id":"C","text":"30 MB/s; raising compute cannot remove the unchanged network bottleneck.","why":"The minimum sustained limit bounds the complete transfer under the supplied assumptions."},{"id":"D","text":"60 MB/s because the network uses one channel for reading and one for writing.","why":"No such extra independent bandwidth is supplied in the scenario."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"Tune from measured bottlenecks. More concurrency or compute does not imply proportional throughput when another component is already limiting the transfer.","videoSeconds":1207,"refs":["pperf","pmonitor"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p29","sourceId":"P29","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Connections","title":"Sequence an on-premises connection","prompt":"A supported SQL Server source is reachable only within the corporate network. A Fabric pipeline must read it without exposing the database publicly. Arrange these steps.","type":"sequence","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure a Fabric on-premises connection with the gateway cluster and supported source credentials.","why":"The configured gateway is the bridge used by the connection to reach the source."},{"id":"B","text":"Select this connection in the source activity and configure the authorized query and destination.","why":"The activity consumes the prepared connection rather than bypassing the network boundary."},{"id":"C","text":"Install and configure a supported on-premises gateway with network access to the source and required outbound service connectivity.","why":"Establish the bridge before creating a working connection through it."},{"id":"D","text":"Run a bounded validation load and inspect connectivity, row counts, and the result.","why":"Test the completed setup with execution evidence before depending on it."}],"correct":["C","A","B","D"],"explanation":"A gateway provides network connectivity; source credentials and the query still determine permitted access. Avoid translating this into an ADF self-hosted integration-runtime requirement.","videoSeconds":369,"refs":["pgateway","psql"],"code":"","origin":"video","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p30","sourceId":"P30","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Lifecycle","title":"Distinguish orchestration and promotion","prompt":"An engineer says a Data Factory pipeline's Copy and Notebook activities will automatically promote a semantic model from development to production. The project separately needs versioned item definitions and controlled environment promotion. Which correction is appropriate?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use data pipelines for runtime orchestration, and the appropriate Git/deployment lifecycle workflow for item promotion.","why":"Runtime processing and promoting analytics assets between environments are distinct responsibilities."},{"id":"B","text":"Export the semantic model to a file and manually overwrite production, treating that file as version history for every referenced Fabric item.","why":"A semantic-model export does not provide version control and controlled promotion for every referenced item."},{"id":"C","text":"Treat Git synchronization alone as proof production ran and validated a new batch.","why":"Source synchronization does not prove the runtime outcome of the changed workflow."},{"id":"D","text":"Keep item definitions only in development and use production connection parameters during data runs as the entire promotion process.","why":"Changing runtime destinations does not maintain the analytics item definitions in the production environment."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"The word pipeline is used for both data execution and deployment workflows. Trace the required outcome before selecting one.","videoSeconds":852,"refs":["pcicd","exam"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":null,"sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p31","sourceId":"P31","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Retail case","title":"Choose the batch architecture","prompt":"Northstar Retail needs immutable raw retention, analyst-owned joins/aggregation, and validated publication. Which architecture best fits all three requirements?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Parse the CSV directly into the report cache and skip raw retention to reduce latency.","why":"The explicit immutable-artifact requirement would be lost."},{"id":"B","text":"Use a pipeline to retain the artifact, prepare the snapshot with the analyst's transformation, validate, and then publish through success gates.","why":"The orchestration coordinates the distinct retention, preparation, and release contracts."},{"id":"C","text":"Use Copy Mapping for supplier joins and regional aggregates, then refresh independently.","why":"The mapping surface does not implement the rowset work and independent refresh ignores readiness."},{"id":"D","text":"Mark the load activity inactive/Succeeded whenever no file is present so reports can release.","why":"An assigned status is not proof that a current batch arrived or validated."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Build the consumer gate around a validated business batch while preserving its source evidence. Raw retention and curated replacement need not use the same write behavior.","videoSeconds":125,"refs":["pcopy","pactivity","pfail"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-northstar-retail","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p32","sourceId":"P32","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Retail case","title":"Select snapshot semantics","prompt":"Northstar's curated table must represent the entire latest snapshot, including removed inventory keys. A full validated snapshot is ready; no concurrent writer exists. Which action is the most direct fit?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Append the full snapshot because immutable raw retention also requires every curated version to accumulate.","why":"Immutable raw artifacts do not require the current curated table to accumulate repeated snapshots."},{"id":"B","text":"Upsert current rows without handling absent keys, because it automatically removes all missing inventory.","why":"Insert/update semantics alone do not delete omitted destination keys."},{"id":"C","text":"Replace the curated current snapshot after validation while retaining the immutable raw batch separately.","why":"Replacement reflects deletions; separate retained artifacts preserve the audit trail."},{"id":"D","text":"Copy the CSV as Binary into Tables and rely on report filters to interpret inventory changes.","why":"Binary preservation does not produce the parsed current-state table required by the reports."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"Specify the target as current state or history before selecting write behavior. This case requires both, on different storage surfaces.","videoSeconds":1134,"refs":["plake"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-northstar-retail","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p33","sourceId":"P33","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Retail case","title":"Protect the release checkpoint","prompt":"The snapshot is copied and transformed, but supplier join validation detects unmatched required keys. Select TWO appropriate decisions.","type":"multi","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Block report release until the required validation passes.","why":"The business readiness contract requires a validated batch, not merely a completed copy."},{"id":"B","text":"Make refresh depend on transformation Completed to preserve the promised freshness time.","why":"This condition can release after failure and does not implement the required business predicate."},{"id":"C","text":"Discard the immutable artifact because validation failure proves the source file is irrelevant.","why":"Retained batch evidence is useful for diagnosis and correction; invalid output does not negate its retention contract."},{"id":"D","text":"Use an explicit invalid-data failure path with a diagnostic code and retain batch identity for investigation.","why":"A deliberate failure outcome communicates the readiness gap and links investigation to its source batch."}],"correct":["A","D"],"explanation":"The publication checkpoint must represent quality, not just movement. Preserve the artifact needed to explain the failed batch.","videoSeconds":1275,"refs":["pfail","plookup"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-northstar-retail","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p34","sourceId":"P34","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Retail case","title":"Handle duplicate delivery","prompt":"Northstar receives the same batch twice through different RunIds. Copy may commit before its acknowledgement is lost. Which design best meets the replay contract?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Deduplicate only on RunId and append every accepted snapshot.","why":"New runs for the same batch have different IDs, and repeated append accumulates snapshots."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppress retries and assume a lost acknowledgement means the destination never changed.","why":"An acknowledgement loss does not prove the absence of a committed write."},{"id":"C","text":"Mark each event successful before loading so duplicate events can be ignored immediately.","why":"A premature success record can permanently skip a batch whose load then fails."},{"id":"D","text":"Use stable batch identity and a serialized, replay-safe replace/publish design; record success only after the validated commit.","why":"The design separates attempt identity from batch identity and handles uncertain prior completion."}],"correct":["D"],"explanation":"An event is a start signal, not an exactly-once guarantee. The replay-safe contract here is an engineering extension beyond the video's demo.","videoSeconds":475,"refs":["pruns","plake"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-northstar-retail","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p35","sourceId":"P35","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Finance case","title":"Choose the closed incremental interval","prompt":"Which source predicate copies exactly Contoso's captured interval and excludes both the prior watermark and rows arriving above the upper bound?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"OrderId > 500 AND OrderId <= 800","why":"Both boundaries match the explicitly defined closed-at-top incremental interval."},{"id":"B","text":"OrderId >= 500 AND OrderId <= 800","why":"ID 500 is already included in the successful prior load, so this overlaps the boundary."},{"id":"C","text":"OrderId > 500","why":"Rows above the captured upper bound could enter this batch and invalidate its control record."},{"id":"D","text":"OrderId > 500 AND OrderId < 800","why":"This omits the defined upper-bound row, creating a gap if the watermark advances to 800."}],"correct":["A"],"explanation":"Freeze the batch's upper bound and carry its exact interval through extraction and commit. The insert-only, strictly increasing source assumptions are essential.","videoSeconds":402,"refs":["pincrement"],"code":"SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE /* select the predicate */;","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-contoso-finance","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p36","sourceId":"P36","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Finance case","title":"Advance only after durable success","prompt":"Arrange Contoso's incremental batch stages so the watermark cannot skip data when copying fails.","type":"sequence","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Commit the validated target interval and update its success watermark atomically using the supplied target-side mechanism.","why":"The control record follows durable validated data; the stated transaction avoids a split publication outcome."},{"id":"B","text":"Copy precisely the bounded interval into batch-isolated staging.","why":"Staging preserves an inspectable candidate rather than altering the publication checkpoint early."},{"id":"C","text":"Read the previous watermark and capture the stable new upper bound.","why":"These inputs define the batch before extraction starts."},{"id":"D","text":"Validate staged row counts, key range, and required business checks.","why":"The candidate is checked before it becomes the published target interval."}],"correct":["C","B","D","A"],"explanation":"Control advancement represents completed work. Atomic replacement/publication is explicitly supplied by this case, not an automatic property of a Copy activity.","videoSeconds":402,"refs":["pincrement","pfail"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-contoso-finance","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p37","sourceId":"P37","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Finance case","title":"Derive a lost-acknowledgement replay","prompt":"All 300 rows for IDs 501–800 were committed, but the acknowledgement was lost before the caller knew the outcome. An unrestricted retry appends the same 300 rows. The target has no enforced uniqueness check. How many physical rows are now present?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"500, because a failed acknowledgement rolls back the already committed interval.","why":"The scenario explicitly says the rows were committed; acknowledgement failure does not undo them."},{"id":"B","text":"1,100, unless the retry contract prevents the duplicate interval.","why":"The original 500 plus the committed 300 plus the replayed 300 yields 1,100 physical rows."},{"id":"C","text":"800, because the activity name identifies the input and automatically deduplicates it.","why":"No activity-name deduplication guarantee is provided for this append operation."},{"id":"D","text":"300, because every append implicitly replaces the target table.","why":"Append retains existing rows; it does not mean table replacement."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"Unknown outcome is not the same as no write. Reconcile or use the supplied replay-safe interval replacement before retrying.","videoSeconds":923,"refs":["pincrement","plake"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-contoso-finance","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p38","sourceId":"P38","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Finance case","title":"Preserve other historical intervals","prompt":"Contoso must retry its uncertain interval without erasing IDs 1–500 or duplicating IDs 501–800. Which design fits the explicitly supplied target-side capability?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Overwrite the whole target with the staged 300-row interval.","why":"This discards the 500 prior rows that the case requires preserving."},{"id":"B","text":"Advance the watermark to 800 before deciding whether the first commit occurred.","why":"That can make missing rows unreachable if the uncertain outcome turns out to be uncommitted."},{"id":"C","text":"Validate and transactionally replace only IDs 501–800, publishing that interval's control record in the same transaction.","why":"Replacing the bounded interval is replay-safe under the supplied isolation and no-other-writer assumptions."},{"id":"D","text":"Append the interval whenever a new pipeline RunId is seen.","why":"A new execution ID does not show whether the business interval was already committed."}],"correct":["C"],"explanation":"The case supplies an atomic interval mechanism. The correct solution uses that contract rather than assuming Copy automatically implements distributed exactly-once delivery.","videoSeconds":923,"refs":["pincrement","pfail"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-contoso-finance","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p39","sourceId":"P39","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Platform case","title":"Promote without a wrong connection","prompt":"Alpine promotes its item definitions, but the production Copy still resolves DEV-ID. The production connection field is parameterized. Select TWO corrective steps.","type":"multi","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Supply PROD-ID through the supported connection parameter in the production configuration.","why":"The connection reference must resolve to the intended production resource in that environment."},{"id":"B","text":"Grant the production execution identity access to DEV-ID and retain that identifier as the production connection parameter.","why":"Access to development does not make it the intended PROD-ID resource binding."},{"id":"C","text":"Run a successful connectivity test with DEV-ID and treat it as sufficient proof that the deployment targets production.","why":"A working connection can still target the wrong environment; verify the effective resource identity and binding."},{"id":"D","text":"Validate the effective connection and execution identity's production access before enabling production runs.","why":"Resolution and authorization must both match the target environment after promotion."}],"correct":["A","D"],"explanation":"Promote definitions deliberately and validate environment-specific bindings. A data-orchestration graph does not automatically satisfy lifecycle promotion requirements.","videoSeconds":852,"refs":["pcicd","pparams"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-alpine-analytics","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null},{"id":"fb-p40","sourceId":"P40","pack":"pipelines","topic":"Platform case","title":"Coordinate isolated loads and validation","prompt":"Alpine must retain twelve-way concurrency, pass the notebook its batch input, and refresh only after every required entity succeeds. Which design satisfies all three constraints?","type":"single","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Set one shared parent entity variable, invoke all children without waiting, and run refresh when the ForEach starts.","why":"Shared mutable naming and nonwaiting release do not establish isolated inputs or successful completion."},{"id":"B","text":"Disable Sequential, set Batch count = 12, pass @item() fields into waiting child calls, gate notebook validation on all-load success, bind batch_id explicitly, and release refresh only after validation passes.","why":"The configured concurrency cap, isolated iteration inputs, waiting barrier, and explicit notebook inputs satisfy the stated release requirements."},{"id":"C","text":"Replace all entity loads with an inactive/Succeeded placeholder and keep the notebook's demo default.","why":"Assigned success performs no required loads and the notebook input remains incorrect."},{"id":"D","text":"Use Completion after the load container and a fixed delay before refresh, leaving notebook base parameters unset.","why":"A failed load can satisfy Completion, elapsed time is not quality, and the notebook lacks the production input."}],"correct":["B"],"explanation":"A parallel workflow needs both isolated per-item inputs and a complete readiness barrier. Batch-level validation and consumer release belong after that barrier.","videoSeconds":306,"refs":["pforeach","pnotebook","pfail"],"code":"","origin":"extension","difficulty":null,"caseStudyId":"case-study-alpine-analytics","sourceObjective":null,"provenance":null,"validation":null,"sourceVideoUrl":null}]
